          "body": "Foveated rendering, edge bundling, and image-based crowd rendering all make controlled tradeoffs between visual utility and complete information. Foveated rendering intentionally lowers peripheral quality to preserve perceived quality; edge bundling reveals coarse connectivity while overdraw and distorted directions obscure individual edges; crowd impostors reduce geometric complexity and texture memory while introducing concerns such as viewpoint-change popping."
        },
        {
          "body": "The shared concept is not simply optimization, but acceptable loss. These systems identify what must remain legible or perceptually convincing, then choose where detail can be compressed, approximated, merged, or regionally reduced. The evidence suggests that graphics methods often succeed by matching loss to the task: comfort and perceived quality in VR, coarse connectivity in graphs, and dense animated presence in virtual cities."
        },
        {
          "body": "A balanced treatment should preserve the caveat that each source names different risks. Foveated rendering is concerned with latency, HVS modeling, and integration into rendering paradigms; edge bundling warns about destroyed edge-level information; crowd rendering addresses popping, texture memory, avatar variety, and shadow consistency."
